::  name       tablecloth error paths
::  shape      paths -- int (@u32), the set's size rather than a bound on it
::  invariant  every refusal the store can make wears a name, and this desk names them all
::  example    9
::  readers    Tablecloth - Pond - Glow Tend - shape museum
::  nib        tablecloth-v1 at 20260812.142132
::
::  Tablecloth's third pedestal, and the first in this room whose number counts NAMES rather
::  than bounding a quantity. brushstroke/tablecloth.rye publishes `ClothError` as the whole
::  set of refusals its store can make, and the count below is that set's size.
::
::  the refusal paths, as ClothError declares them:
::    NameEmpty - NameTooLong - NameHasSeparator - NameTaken - UnknownArtifact -
::    CatalogFull - ContentTooLarge - Overflow - BadManifest
::  that is the whole set.
::
::  Why this desk names them rather than only counting them. A count agrees while the set
::  underneath it has changed: rename one path, drop one and add another, and nine is still
::  nine. So the guard beside this desk reads both sides as SETS -- the names written above
::  and the names ClothError declares -- and compares them sorted, then compares the count to
::  the set's own size. The count is the fact a reader takes in at the door; the names are the
::  fact that keeps it honest.
::
::  This is one layer up from the lesson the store learned on 20260830: a bound on a name's
::  LENGTH was read as a bound on its ALPHABET, and the round-trip the module promised was
::  false for every name carrying a space. A count read as a set is the same substitution --
::  the size of a thing standing in for the thing.
::
::  What this desk does not reach. Whether nine is the right number of ways to refuse, which
::  is Tablecloth's own design question; and what each path means, which the Rye's own comment
::  beside each `return error.X` says. A pedestal displays what the source decided.
+$  tablecloth-error-paths-shape
  $:  paths=@u32
  ==
